prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages smart-building automation and zone control.

The people involved are facility managers, zone controllers, maintenance techs, and tenants. One person may hold both the facility manager and zone controller role at the same time.

The resources are actuators, zones, and commission requests. Every actuator records its owner, zone group, device tags, maintenance flag, and status, every zone records its controller, criticality, and status, and every commission request records its device, requester, and status.

Each requester carries a control tier and membership information.

Each request carries the current time, whether occupancy is detected, whether it comes from the on-site building network, and whether there is a maintenance override.

A facility manager may approve a commission request only while it is in requested status.

A facility manager must not approve a commission request that is not in requested status.

A facility manager may approve a commission request only when the request comes from the on-site building network, and must not approve it from any other network.

A facility manager may approve a commission request only when the request falls within a permitted window, and must not approve it outside that window.

A request by a facility manager to approve a commission that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity User {
    roles: Set<String>,
    controlTier: Long,
    memberships: Set<String>,
};

entity Zone {
    controller: User,
    criticality: String,
    status: String,
};

entity Actuator {
    owner: User,
    zoneGroup: Zone,
    deviceTags: Set<String>,
    maintenanceFlag: Bool,
    status: String,
};

entity CommissionRequest {
    device: Actuator,
    requester: User,
    status: String,
};

action approveCommissionRequest appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [CommissionRequest],
    context: {
        currentTime: datetime,
        occupancyDetected: Bool,
        fromOnSiteBuildingNetwork: Bool,
        maintenanceOverride: Bool,
        withinPermittedWindow: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
